## Why monitors
Semaphores are powerful but error-prone: swap two waits or forget a signal and you get deadlock or races, and nothing ties the semaphore to the data it protects. A monitor is a language-level construct: shared data plus the procedures that access it, with implicit mutual exclusion (only one thread active inside) and condition variables for waiting.

## Parts
- Entry lock: entering any method acquires the monitor's lock; leaving releases it.
- Condition variables with wait() (release the lock and sleep) and signal()/notify() (wake a waiter).
- Java: synchronized methods plus wait/notify/notifyAll on the object; ReentrantLock with Condition is the explicit form.

## Signal semantics
- Hoare monitors: signal hands the lock to the waiter immediately; the condition is guaranteed true on wakeup. Elegant, hard to implement.
- Mesa monitors (Java, pthreads, Go sync.Cond): signal only marks the waiter runnable; it must re-acquire the lock later and the condition may have changed. Therefore always:
```java
synchronized (this) {
    while (!condition) wait();   // loop, not if
    ...
    notifyAll();                 // or notify() if safe
}
```

## Example: bounded buffer monitor (Java)
```java
synchronized void put(T x) throws InterruptedException {
    while (count == cap) wait();
    buf[tail] = x; tail = (tail+1)%cap; count++;
    notifyAll();
}
synchronized T take() throws InterruptedException {
    while (count == 0) wait();
    T x = buf[head]; head = (head+1)%cap; count--;
    notifyAll();
    return x;
}
```

## Pitfalls
- notify() with waiters on different conditions can wake the wrong thread and lose progress; prefer notifyAll or separate Condition objects (notFull, notEmpty).
- Lost wakeup: signal before the waiter waits. A predicate loop plus holding the lock while changing the condition prevents it.
- Nested monitors can deadlock; calling unknown code (callbacks) while holding the lock is dangerous.
- Holding the lock during slow I/O destroys concurrency.

## Follow-ups
- wait() must be called holding the lock; otherwise IllegalMonitorStateException (Java) or undefined behavior (pthreads).
- Monitor vs mutex plus condvar: same mechanism; a monitor enforces the structure in the language.
